---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { cards, systemCards, benchmarks, labs, EPOCH_SOURCE, SATURATED, AS_OF } from '../lib/data.ts';
import { benchmarkCount } from '../lib/counts.ts';
import { LEVELS, SCORING } from '../lib/taxonomy.ts';
import { fullDate } from '../lib/format.ts';
import reviewed from '../../data/reviewed.json';
import sources from '../../data/sources.json';
import Criteria from '../components/Criteria.astro';
import { STAYS_OUT } from '../lib/criteria.ts';

const leftOut = (reviewed as { rejected?: unknown[] }).rejected?.length ?? 0;
const readDaily = (sources as { automated?: boolean }[]).filter((s) => s.automated).length;
---

<Base title="How this works" description="Where our data comes from, and the rules we use to name models and label benchmarks.">
  <div class="page prose">
    <header class="head">
      <h1>How this works</h1>
      <p class="lede">We collect scores from lab model cards, official leaderboards and independent runs, and match each one to a single name for its model. We build the site from data files in our GitHub repository.</p>
    </header>

    <section id="cataloged">
      <h2>What gets cataloged</h2>
      <p>
        We count a named test of AI models as a benchmark if anyone can run it again. It can be for language models, agents, vision and audio models, robot policies or world models. We add one to the catalog if it passes <b>at least one</b> of four tests.
      </p>
      <Criteria />
      <p>
        <b>What stays out:</b> {STAYS_OUT}
      </p>
      <p>
        We've checked and left out {leftOut.toLocaleString('en-US')} finds so far, and the <a href="/new/">New page</a> lists each one with our reason. A find that fails now can pass later, once a lab reports it or people start using it. To suggest one, use the <a href="/submit/">submission form</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="data">
      <h2>The data</h2>
      <p>
        <b>Lab model cards.</b> {cards.length} launch posts from {new Set(cards.map((c) => c.lab)).size} labs, from March 2023 to {fullDate(AS_OF)}. For each post we record the benchmarks in its headline results and the model's score on each, as the post gives it. Whether we call a benchmark in use, fading or dropped depends only on these posts.
        {systemCards.length > 0 && ` We also read ${systemCards.length} system cards, model cards and technical reports that came out with the releases. From these we record every evaluation each document reports, safety and frontier-risk tests included, along with the section each result appears in.`}
        Labs pick their own settings, so card scores don't compare as cleanly as independent runs. We say on each page which kind of score it shows.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Official leaderboards.</b> Many benchmarks keep their own leaderboard, and new models usually show up there first. We import each board from the data file it publishes, using the scripts in <code>scripts/leaderboards/</code>, and match its model names to ours. If a benchmark's own board has five or more models, we lead with it on that benchmark's page.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Independent runs.</b> Scores from <a href={EPOCH_SOURCE.url}>{EPOCH_SOURCE.name}</a> ({EPOCH_SOURCE.license}), retrieved {fullDate(EPOCH_SOURCE.retrieved)}. Epoch runs some of these itself and collects the rest from public leaderboards. We also use its Epoch Capabilities Index, which puts models on one scale across many benchmarks. We leave out the rows Epoch collected from Artificial Analysis, because Artificial Analysis's terms do not allow reuse in other ranking products.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>The catalog.</b> {benchmarkCount.toLocaleString('en-US')} benchmarks ({benchmarks.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} counting each version), each with what it tests, who made it, and links to its code, paper, data and leaderboard. We checked every GitHub link against the GitHub API. The list began with benchmarks from lab cards, Epoch's hub and public collections, and research agents filled in the details from primary sources.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="names">
      <h2>One name per model</h2>
      <p>
        We match every score to one record in <code>data/models.json</code>, whatever the source called the model. Each record has the model's name as its lab writes it, its lab and release date, whether its weights are open, and the other spellings it goes by. If a lab shipped several versions under one name, each version gets its own record and a snapshot label, such as GPT-4o (May 2024). Reasoning effort and harness are stored as settings on a score, so GPT-5 at high effort is still GPT-5. We rank a model on a benchmark by its best setting.
      </p>
      <p>We do the same for the {labs.length} labs and for benchmarks. Each has one record with its aliases, so "GPQA-D" in one card and "GPQA Diamond" in another count as one benchmark. Versions don't merge, so ARC-AGI-2 has a record of its own.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="labels">
      <h2>The labels</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>In use</dt>
        <dd>At least two model cards reported it in the last six months, or the first card to report it is less than six months old.</dd>
        <dt>Fading</dt>
        <dd>Not in use, but a card reported it in the last year.</dd>
        <dt>Dropped</dt>
        <dd>No card has reported it for a year.</dd>
        <dt>Saturated</dt>
        <dd>The best score has passed {SATURATED}%. If a dropped benchmark's best score had already passed {SATURATED}% when the last card reported it, we say labs dropped it after it saturated.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section id="levels">
      <h2>Levels and scoring</h2>
      <p>Each benchmark gets a level for who could solve its tasks. Model scores play no part in it.</p>
      <dl>
        {LEVELS.map((l) => (
          <>
            <dt>{l.name}</dt>
            <dd>{l.who}.</dd>
          </>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="gap">We also record how each benchmark is scored. Votes and matches rank models against each other, and every other method scores them against a fixed standard.</p>
      <dl>
        {SCORING.map((m) => (
          <>
            <dt>{m.name}</dt>
            <dd>{m.how}</dd>
          </>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section id="versions">
      <h2>Versions</h2>
      <p>
        When a benchmark comes out in versions, such as Terminal-Bench 1.0 to 4.0 or PostTrainBench 1.0 to 1.2, we keep one record per version and link them as a family. We never mix scores across versions. A card that reports "PostTrainBench v1.1" counts toward 1.1 only. The benchmarks list shows each family once, at its newest version, and each version's page links to the others.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="leaders">
      <h2>Who held number one</h2>
      <p>
        A model takes number one on a benchmark on the day its score beats every earlier score, and holds it until a later model beats it. If two models tie, the earlier one keeps the spot. We date a score to its model's release, or a lab claim to the card that made it. No score is dated before the benchmark came out. Our charts start in January 2023.
      </p>
      <p>
        We stop counting time at number one once the best score passes {SATURATED}%, since a lead means little after that. We also stop if more than 45 days go by with no newer model tested, because then nobody knows who would lead. In that case the count ends on the date of the newest model tested. The first model to take a benchmark past {SATURATED}% gets a close-out, which we count separately. A new version doesn't end the count for the old one. As long as people still test the old version its race goes on, and ARC-AGI-1 wasn't closed out until long after ARC-AGI-2 came out. Shares of time at number one include only these contested days.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="finding">
      <h2>Finding new benchmarks</h2>
      <p>
        Every morning we automatically read {readDaily} of the sources on our <a href="/sources/">Sources page</a>. We score each find in two stages, first on whether it is a benchmark at all, then on the evidence that it passes one of the four tests above. Finds that score 8 or more go to the <a href="/new/">review queue</a>. Finds that score 5 to 7 go on a watch list and drop off after 14 days with no new mention. We skip anything we've already left out, and we never scrape X, because its terms forbid it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before a find joins the catalog, a person or a research agent checks it against its own paper, repository or leaderboard. The agents also look for newer versions of benchmarks we already have.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="credits">
      <h2>Credits</h2>
      <p>
        Scores and the capability index: Epoch AI, under CC BY 4.0. Paper abstracts: arXiv metadata, under CC0, and other papers under the license each one names. Lab marks: <a href="https://github.com/lobehub/lobe-icons">LobeHub Icons</a>, under the MIT License, and the labs’ own websites and GitHub or Hugging Face organizations. The marks are trademarks of their owners. Type: Newsreader and Geist, under the SIL Open Font License. Our page designs borrow from the benchmark pages of Epoch AI, Artificial Analysis, Arena and Design Arena.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose section {
    max-width: 68ch;
  }
  .prose section + section {
    margin-top: 64px;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: 30px;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .prose p + p {
    margin-top: 14px;
  }
  .prose p {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .prose b {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .gap {
    margin-top: 28px;
  }
  section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: 90px;
  }
  dl {
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 170px 1fr;
    gap: 12px 24px;
  }
  dt {
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.88em;
    background: var(--panel);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
</style>
